<p>Utilitarianism, Chapters 3 and 4</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/gettyimages-168956792-612x612.jpg" alt="Photograph of hands reaching out to one another in a gesture of mutual help." width="860" height="320" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Two questions occupy these chapters: why should anyone be motivated to act morally, and can the principle of utility itself be defended? Both questions are harder than they look.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    External and internal sanctions
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Chapter 3 turns from <em>what is right</em> to <em>what motivates people to do right</em>. Mill uses the word "sanction" for the source of moral motivation: whatever pressure, internal or external, gets us to act in accordance with morality. He distinguishes two kinds.</p>
<p><strong>External sanctions</strong> are pressures imposed from outside the person. The most obvious are legal: do this and you go to jail. But there are softer external sanctions too. Social pressure, whether the disapproval of your community, the loss of standing among your friends, or the threat of public shame, can be just as powerful as legal threats. Religious sanctions, where they are believed, add the prospect of divine punishment or reward. And there are reputational sanctions, the long-run consequences of being known as the kind of person who does or does not do certain things.</p>
<p>Mill thinks external sanctions matter but are not the whole story. A morality that only worked because of external pressure would collapse the moment external pressure was removed. People do what is right even when no one is watching, and Mill wants to explain why.</p>
<p>His answer is <strong>internal sanctions</strong>: the pressures we exert on ourselves. The most important is conscience, the sense of guilt or self-reproach we feel when we have done wrong, even if no one else knows. Mill thinks conscience is not a separate moral faculty given to us in advance (the way it might be for a religious thinker) but a learned, cultivated response. As we grow up in a moral community, we internalize that community's standards and come to feel guilt when we violate them. The feeling is real, but its source is social and developmental rather than metaphysical.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    The social feelings of humankind
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Behind the internal sanctions, Mill thinks, lies something even more basic: our natural sociability. Humans are constitutionally social creatures. We need others to survive in infancy, we depend on cooperative arrangements for almost everything in adulthood, and we have evolved deep psychological tendencies to identify with the people around us. Sympathy with others' joy and pain is not a moral achievement we have to argue for; it is a feature of human psychology that shows up reliably in normally developed people.</p>
<p>This social nature is what allows the principle of utility to motivate us in the first place. We are not just being told to count everyone's happiness equally as some abstract requirement. We are being told to take seriously something we already take seriously: the well-being of the people we live among. The principle of utility extends and systematizes a feeling we already have. It does not create the feeling out of nothing.</p>
<p>This explains why Mill thinks moral motivation does not require a special faculty or a divine commandment. We act morally because we are creatures who naturally care about other people, and the principle of utility is the systematic working-out of what that care commits us to. The external sanctions exist as backups for the cases where the natural feelings are insufficient or have been damaged, but the foundation is the social feeling itself.</p>
<p>It is worth noting how different this is from what we have seen in earlier units. Aristotle grounded morality in the function of a human being. Aquinas grounded it in the natural law God built into creation. Kant grounded it in the rational structure of the will. Mill grounds it in human sentiment: in the natural emotional responses of social creatures to one another. This is a much more naturalistic foundation, less ambitious metaphysically, and it leaves moral judgment more dependent on contingent facts about human psychology. Whether that is a strength or a weakness is one of the central debates about utilitarianism.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Mill's "proof" of the principle of utility
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Chapter 4 takes up the question Mill has been avoiding throughout the book: what reason is there to accept the principle of utility itself? Why think happiness is the right standard for moral judgment, rather than some other candidate?</p>
<p>Mill begins with a concession. Ultimate principles cannot be proved in the strict sense, because proof always works by deriving a conclusion from something more basic, and an ultimate principle by definition has nothing more basic to derive it from. So whatever Mill is going to offer is not a strict logical proof. It is something looser: considerations that, if taken together, make it reasonable to accept the principle of utility as the foundation of morality.</p>
<p>What he offers is, essentially, the following argument. The only evidence we can have that something is <em>visible</em> is that people actually see it. The only evidence we can have that something is <em>audible</em> is that people actually hear it. By analogy, Mill says, the only evidence we can have that something is <em>desirable</em> is that people actually desire it. Now: everyone desires their own happiness. Therefore each person's happiness is good (to that person). Therefore the general happiness is good (in the aggregate, taking everyone into account). And this, Mill concludes, gives us the considerations we need to accept the principle of utility.</p>
<p>The argument has been criticized intensely since the day the book was published. The most famous criticism focuses on a single move: Mill's analogy between "visible" and "desirable."</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>A NOTE ON THE ARGUMENT</p>
<p>"Visible" means <em>able to be seen</em>. "Audible" means <em>able to be heard</em>. The fact that people see something is evidence that the thing is visible, because being visible just is being the kind of thing that can be seen.</p>
<p>"Desirable" does not work the same way. "Desirable" does not mean <em>able to be desired</em>. It means <em>worth desiring</em>, or <em>ought to be desired</em>. Almost anything is <em>able</em> to be desired: people desire harmful drugs, vengeance, the suffering of their enemies. The fact that something is desired (in fact, by someone) does not establish that it is desirable (worth being desired, ought to be desired). The two senses come apart.</p>
<p>Mill's argument seems to slide between the two senses. He needs to get from "people desire happiness" (a fact about what people in fact want) to "happiness is desirable" (a claim about what we ought to value). The analogy with "visible" only works if "desirable" means "able to be desired," and that is not the sense Mill needs for the argument to deliver a moral conclusion.</p>
<p>This is a paradigm case of what is sometimes called <strong>equivocation</strong>: using the same word in two different senses across an argument, in a way that makes the inference look valid when it is not. It is also closely related to what G. E. Moore would later call <em>the naturalistic fallacy</em>: the attempt to derive an evaluative conclusion ("X is good") from purely descriptive premises ("people in fact want X"). Whether all such derivations are fallacious is itself a contested question, but Mill's particular move here is widely regarded as a clear case of the problem.</p>
<p>Notice that pointing out this error does not by itself refute utilitarianism. It refutes only the specific argument Mill offers for it. A philosophical theory and the arguments offered in its defense are different things; an unsuccessful argument leaves the theory unproven but does not establish that it is false. Many philosophers continue to defend utilitarianism on better grounds than Mill provided here.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    The aggregation problem
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>There is a second move in Mill's proof that is worth flagging, because it surfaces a deeper philosophical problem we will return to in Chapter 5.</p>
<p>Mill argues: each person desires their own happiness, so each person's happiness is good <em>to that person</em>; therefore the general happiness is good in the aggregate <em>to all of us collectively</em>. This step looks innocent but actually does a lot of work. It moves from claims about what individual people want for themselves to claims about what is good from a collective, impersonal point of view.</p>
<p>Why is this controversial? Because each person desiring their own happiness does not obviously commit each person to caring about everyone's happiness. I can intensely value my own well-being and still feel no particular concern for the well-being of strangers. The fact that my happiness is a good <em>to me</em>, and your happiness is a good <em>to you</em>, does not by itself produce the conclusion that an impersonal sum of happiness across both of us is a good <em>that anyone is obligated to promote</em>. There is something missing in the inference.</p>
<p>Some philosophers think this gap can be bridged with additional argument; others think it cannot be bridged at all, and that consequentialist theories ultimately have to take "the good is to be impartially promoted" as an unargued first principle rather than something derivable from individual desires. Either way, this is a place where Mill's proof leans on an assumption that does much more work than it appears to do.</p>
<p>The aggregation problem becomes acute in Chapter 5, where Mill takes up justice. The cases where aggregation feels most morally problematic are cases where the sum of small benefits to many people seems to justify a large harm to one. Justice, on most accounts, is what protects individuals against being used as means to others' greater welfare. We will see in the next lecture how Mill tries to handle this.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    What this means for utilitarianism overall
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>It is worth pausing on what Chapter 4 has and has not accomplished.</p>
<p>What it has done: Mill has shown that we need some grounding for moral principles, that the kind of grounding available is not strict logical proof, and that the principle of utility has some considerations in its favor (people in fact value happiness, happiness is the kind of thing whose pursuit feels self-justifying, and so on).</p>
<p>What it has not done: Mill has not given a watertight argument that the principle of utility is true. His central proof has a serious flaw (the desired/desirable equivocation), and his move from individual to aggregate well-being papers over a real philosophical problem. The proof is suggestive rather than conclusive.</p>
<p>Most contemporary utilitarians acknowledge this and look for better grounds. Some argue that utilitarianism's appeal lies not in any logical derivation but in its impartiality (it treats everyone's interests equally) and its practical applicability (it gives clear guidance in cases where other theories give none). Others argue that the principle of utility captures something deep about what morality is for: making lives go better is the kind of thing morality should be about, even if we cannot prove this from outside morality.</p>
<p>For your purposes in this course, the important takeaways are these. Mill defends utilitarianism with one of the most famously flawed arguments in the history of philosophy. The flaw is real and worth understanding. But the flaw in a single argument does not refute the theory, and utilitarianism remains one of the major ethical traditions, with serious defenders to this day. When you evaluate utilitarianism in your reflection and your final paper, judge the theory itself, not just Mill's particular defense of it.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>Chapter 5 brings us to justice, the part of morality that consequentialism has the hardest time accommodating. If happiness is what matters, why do we think it is wrong to convict an innocent person even when the conviction would prevent a riot? Why do rights feel like absolute claims rather than rules of thumb? Mill's answer is one of the most ambitious moves in the book.</p>
</div></div>
